{{ contents([{"id": "start", "title": "Find your starting point"}, {"id": "sample-check", "title": "Create and back up the disposable file"}, {"id": "external-storage", "title": "Restore with the platform's own route"}, {"id": "cloud-storage", "title": "Treat cloud retrieval as a separate case"}, {"id": "inspect-and-record", "title": "Inspect the result and record the limits"}, {"id": "related", "title": "What to read next"}]) }}
<div class="ed-reading guide-reading text-lg">
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A backup is easier to trust when you have followed one complete path from included file to restored copy. This is a deliberately small test, not proof that every folder, version or recovery scenario will work. Use only a disposable plain-text sample containing no personal data.</p>
<section class="ed-start" id="start" aria-labelledby="start-heading"><p class="ed-label">Start here</p><h2 id="start-heading">Set up a safe sample</h2><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">The restore instructions differ between Windows, Mac and cloud services. Do not force one platform's steps onto another.</p><div class="ed-choices">
{{ choice("External storage", "A connected drive may hold the backup, but confirm the completed backup point before starting the restore.", "#external-storage", "startup", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "An external drive beside a folder and document.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Cloud storage", "Use a provider-supported download or export to a separate local destination, not an in-place rollback.", "#cloud-storage", "applications", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "A dark cloud and document above a laptop.", "Read this part") }}
{{ choice("Sample check", "Create, back up, restore and inspect one known text file without deleting or overwriting anything important.", "#sample-check", "online", "/img/guides/backup-essentials-v2.webp", "Two separate documents examined with a magnifying glass.", "Read this part") }}
</div></section>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="sample-check"><span aria-hidden="true">01</span>Create and back up the disposable file</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For File History or Time Machine, choose a new, uniquely named local test folder that your backup includes and that is not cloud-synchronised. Create a new <code>sample.txt</code> inside it with no personal data; if a sample with that name already exists, use a different new folder. Include a fictional label such as “Backup check”, the date and a sentence you will recognise. For a cloud test, keep this original outside synchronised folders and use the provider-supported method to place a copy of this disposable sample in the selected backup. If you cannot identify a safe copy-only method, stop and check the provider instructions.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Keep the original. Wait until the backup system shows that a completed backup contains the sample. If you cannot establish that, stop rather than guessing. Do not delete the file to make the test seem successful.</p>{% call note("care", "Take care", "pause when the route is unclear") %}<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Never use an important document, user folder or real customer data as a test. If the drive is failing, files are unreadable or malware is suspected, pause for assessment before continuing writes.</p>{% endcall %}
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="external-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">02</span>Restore with the platform&#x27;s own route</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On Windows, File History's previous-version flow can offer <em>Restore to...</em>, which is useful for placing the test copy somewhere separate. Avoid an ordinary restore if it would replace the current file; that is not the bounded test you want. See the <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/windows/experience/backup-recovery/backup-and-restore-with-file-history">current Microsoft instructions</a>.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">On Mac, Time Machine normally returns a selected item to its original location. The Windows alternate-location steps do not apply. For this test only, after the backup completes, first check that <code>sample-original-kept.txt</code> does not already exist, then rename only the disposable original to that name, leaving <code>sample.txt</code> unused. Open the test folder, open Time Machine using Spotlight and select a backup containing the sample before the rename. Restore only <code>sample.txt</code> to its original, now-empty path, following <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.apple.com/en-au/guide/mac-help/mh11422/mac">Apple's restore guidance</a>.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If a name conflict or replace prompt appears, cancel. Do not proceed. The rename strategy applies only to this disposable file: never use it on folders or user documents.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="cloud-storage"><span aria-hidden="true">03</span>Treat cloud retrieval as a separate case</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">For a cloud destination, use the provider-supported download or export route to a new local folder or another clearly separate location. Do not test by deleting the original or relying on an in-place rollback. An online-only item may first need internet access.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the provider does not offer a clear, safe way to retrieve this sample as a separate copy, stop and consult its current instructions. <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/onedrive/save-disk-space-with-onedrive-files-on-demand-for-windows">OneDrive synchronises deletions</a>, so a synchronised folder should not be treated as a harmless restore sandbox. A download of the current file tests access to that copy, not older-version recovery or an independent backup.</p>
<h2 class="ed-section-heading" id="inspect-and-record"><span aria-hidden="true">04</span>Inspect the result and record the limits</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Open the restored copy and check the exact known sentence, fictional label and date. Record the test date, source or backup point, destination and outcome. Keep the original and restored copy separate until you are satisfied with the inspection.</p><p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A successful sample shows that this particular file and route worked at that time. It does not establish exhaustive protection, prove that other folders are included or guarantee recovery of applications and system settings. Repeat a bounded check when the backup arrangement changes.</p>
